Sval: a tab moved to another window keeps the default jar

adoptTab gives the new tab the moving tab's usercontextid attribute, and a
tab in the default jar has none, so addTab gives the new tab
gBrowser.defaultUserContextId, which Sval sets to the jar of the workspace
the window shows (workspace-jar-for-new-tabs.patch). A tab dragged into an
isolated workspace's window would take that workspace's jar before the user
has agreed to the move. Asking for jar 0 keeps the tab in its own jar, and
SvalTabs reopens it in the workspace's jar once its node moves there.

diff --git a/browser/components/tabbrowser/content/tabbrowser.js b/browser/components/tabbrowser/content/tabbrowser.js
index bbd319efa9..5d4fb4f492 100644
--- a/browser/components/tabbrowser/content/tabbrowser.js
+++ b/browser/components/tabbrowser/content/tabbrowser.js
@@ -7608,6 +7608,9 @@
         // new tab must have the same usercontextid as the old one
         params.userContextId = aTab.getAttribute("usercontextid");
       }
+      // Sval: and one in the default jar stays in it, where addTab would
+      // give the window's workspace jar.
+      params.userContextId ??= 0;
       params.skipLoad = true;
       let newTab = this.addWebTab("about:blank", params);
 
